━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
_ FunctionEmitter

` Builds one function's IR from its syntax and the facts the type pass recorded for its instance, or
` from the program tables for a function with a generated body. It decides nothing: a construct it
` has no shape for is recorded as a refusal and ends the function. `unit` says whose code is
` emitted and where its locals are (`Unit`). A value emitted
` comes with its type (`Typed`), which every use checks against the type it needs, so a
` conversion nothing applied refuses the function. `held-chain` is the value written after the `?`
` of the statement being emitted, which runs only where an optional holds a value: such a value
` has no shape under any statement that does not name it here. `pins`
` are the expressions whose values were evaluated before the code that reads them: the receiver of a
` link of an optional chain, the value of an assignment made only where an optional holds one, the
` subject a pattern compares, the object a place is stored back into. `budget` is
` the cell that counts the passes of the function's loops down to their next poll, when the function
` polls, and `probe-countdown` the one that counts the polls down to their next look in on the
` probes, where they look. `loops` are the loops whose bodies are being emitted, innermost last, each with
` its item, which is a value. `open-cleanups` are the cleanups open, oldest first, each with how
` many were open where the block that opened it began, which `scoping` keeps for the block being
` emitted (`Scoping`). `use-head` is the block the entry goes on to in a function that has a `use`, emitted
` last, which clears
` `use-flags` and goes on to `use-body`. `cleanup-raised` is the cell of the flag that says a
` cleanup run on a way out no panic made has raised one, and `landings` counts the checks emitted,
` so that a cleanup with none is known. `line-shift` is how many lines below where it is written an
` instruction of the function is located, and `lowered` the feed whose own instructions are
` located one line further down. `placing` says an instruction whose source is in a module other
` than the function's is located in that module's file, as none is otherwise. `lazy-open` are the
` lazy locals whose values are being evaluated. `lent-lazies` are the lazy locals declared inside
` the value of one a function written in the one declaring them evaluates (`open-lent-lazies`).
` `shared-depth` counts the values of lazy locals that more than one read may evaluate
` where the type pass found a read that knows a local the value reads otherwise than the first: such
` a value is typed where it is first read, under what the flow knows there, so nothing in it that
` was settled by the flow has a shape, but what a test the value itself made settled
` (`is-inside-read`), which holds wherever the value is evaluated. `sharing` counts those a function written in the one that
` declares them is evaluating (`read-shared`). `ready-lazies` are the flags of the lazy locals kept
` behind one, and `shared-ready` the lazy locals nested functions capture, each with the block where
` an evaluation of it emitted in the function has ended (`evaluated-before`). `home-owner` is the instance `find-captured` found to declare the slot it was asked for, and
` `home-layout` the layout of the environment that holds it there. `in-zone` is whether the code
` being emitted runs where the collector never sees it: the body of a function marked so, until it
` raises. `zone-answer` says, of the value the last call that raises errors answered where that zone
` has to do with it, what its path does to the zone: 1 nothing, 2 enters it, 3 leaves it; an error
` comes back outside it. It is 0 where the zone has nothing to do with the call, and whoever takes
` the value apart clears it. `block-effects` is what the `do` blocks around the code being emitted
` keep it from (`effects-here`). `given-loop` is the loop of a comprehension, in the function
` generated for it: that function is given the loop's container where it is called, and yields the
` body of the loop for each item. `subscribing` is the `for` block of a loop on a stream, or a comprehension on
` one, in the function generated for it, which is given an item and the stream. `given-pipe` is a
` stream piped through a pattern, in the function generated for it, which is given the stream's
` value and yields what each match gives. `poll-landings` counts the checks of `landings` that a
` loop's poll made. `feeds` is the first feed of a list, in the function generated for it, which
` yields what each feed gives. `piped` is the pipe whose right side the function being emitted was
` generated for: it is given the item, or the value piped, after the environment it was written in.
` `piped-site` is where the type pass keeps what converts a list of feeds or a pipe that is piped
` forward into a call, until that call reads it. `open-turns` are the loops whose turn is being
` emitted and has an environment of its own, innermost last. `turn` and `turn-place` are those of
` the one `find-captured` found a slot in, when it is one of these. `rebindings` are the cells a
` machine's names were bound to anew while the expansion of an exit or an entry that binds them is
` emitted, innermost last, each with the local it held before.
` `through` is the place of the address of that slot in the capture of the
` body of a `do without gc` block, when the capture refers to it. `builds` says the function returns
` its receiver, and `initializes` that the function is a constructor run on an object another made.
` `filling` is the properties a constructor gives their first values (`Filling`), and `making` the
` tuple or struct whose fields are given theirs (`Making`), whose `made` says which fields have
` theirs, as `filled` does for properties. `live-actions` holds the
` trackers of the live actions open where code is emitted, innermost last, and
` `rerunning` says the statement a live action runs again is being emitted in the function that runs
` it. `place-trackers` are the properties whose values a constructor gave in a live action of their
` own, each with its trackers, made where the constructor begins for a value a read may give on some
` paths alone (`filling.trackers`); `attribute-trackers` the attributes of markup elements being made
` whose values were given so, and `argument-trackers` the same of their arguments given by position.
` `branch-trackers` are the trackers of each markup branch or loop region being made, kept until the
` element has taken it.
` `task-leaving` is where a return in the body of a spawned task goes, to end the task.
` `task-failing` is where each landing pad of a task body goes when the task has handlers, and
` `task-handlers` are those handlers; `handling-failure` holds while they are emitted.
` `styling` is what the style rules applied at a site keep (`Styling`), and `rule-givens` the values
` given to the style rules written on the element `valued` names, as evaluated. `rule-names` are the names a style rule
` applied where code is emitted takes its values by. `around-values` are the values the style rules
` written on markup elements gave, for the rules written in them to read, and `taken-values` those
` this function was given (`take-values`). `style-order` is the order the next declaration of a rule
` applied at a site takes. `style-conditions` are the conditions made at the site where code is
` emitted. `site-self`, `site-self-type`, `site-has-self` and `site-deferred` are the receiver where
` the markup element
` whose rules are applied is written, which a condition written on it reads. `site-gates` are the
` gates of the site. `state-cells` are the state
` cells of `scope-cells` made where code is emitted. `bridged` says the function is built as the
` bridge TypeScript makes for a member that runs on a row's case, and `row-columns` is the argument
` that member takes the row's columns in. `this-cursor` is the case of a row such a member's
` receiver stands for. `dispatch-row` is the row the function made for a member a union forwards
` takes, while its cases are called, of the type `dispatch-type`, and `dispatch-columns` the columns
` found where its storage was checked; no type where it takes none. `made-rows` are the rows `t[i]`
` made values. `matching` is the pattern being emitted (`Match`).

